<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Yaudheya republic</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 350 CE (approx.) · capital Rohtak</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A long-lived warrior republic of Haryana and Rajasthan that worshipped the war god Karttikeya and defied Saka and Kushan rulers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Yaudheyas were already known to the grammarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a> as a people who lived by arms. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> they emerged as an independent republic in the 2nd century BCE, holding land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>. Coin moulds found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohtak">Rohtak</a> suggest it was an important centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Yaudheyas were a gana governed by an assembly and an elected leader. Their coins carry the legend Yaudheya ganasya jaya, victory to the Yaudheya republic, and images of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartikeya">Karttikeya</a>, the war god they treated as their divine sovereign. Their reputation for military skill was such that the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saka">Saka</a> ruler Rudradaman boasted in his <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/junagadh-state">Junagadh</a> inscription of 150 of having defeated them.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Yaudheyas reasserted their independence after the decline of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a> in the 3rd century. In the 4th century they submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudragupta">Samudragupta</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> pillar inscription lists them, and they then disappear from history.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 200 BCE – 350 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:55.72%;width:10.95%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 350 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rohtak</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, worship of Karttikeya</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yaudheyas, Yaudheya Gana</dd></dl></aside></div>

